hdump and undump on OS-9 -- what was changed===========================================Mike Haaland's hdump 1.1, 25 January 1996, "Copying-policy: freelydistributable", with OS-9 K&R C named among its platforms.  ORIG/ holds therelease as posted.NOTHING WAS CHANGED.  Both programs compiled as they came -- hdump fromod.c and cnv.c, undump from undump.c -- which is what the author intended:the release lists OS-9 as a target.Two things the README says that this build does not do, recorded herebecause they would otherwise read as faults:1.  HEXADECIMAL IS NOT THE DEFAULT.  The README and the Description both    say "-h hexadecimal display (default)".  Run bare, this build dumps in    OCTAL; `hdump -h' gives the hex-and-characters view.  Measured, not    assumed.2.  undump DOES NOT READ OS-9 `dump' OUTPUT.  The README says it accepts    "HEX output from hdump or from the OS-9 dump utility".  It does not:    fed `dump' output it stops with "bad hex value".  The two layouts    differ -- `dump' heads its columns "Addr" and runs the bytes together,    hdump heads them "Offset" and pairs them differently.  Fed `hdump -h'    output, undump rebuilds the file EXACTLY: 40 bytes in, 40 bytes out,    byte for byte.    So the round trip is hdump -h -> edit -> undump, and that is the    workflow worth knowing: it turns any text editor into a binary editor    on a disk that has none.